# The Dockerfile COPYs exactly one thing -- `agentic/` -- so the build context is
# an allowlist: exclude everything, add back that one directory, then re-exclude
# the artefacts that can appear inside it.
#
# The concrete bug this prevents: running `make lint` or `make parse` on the host
# leaves agentic/lib/__pycache__ behind, and COPY reads the filesystem rather than
# git, so that host bytecode was shipped inside the image -- cpython-314 .pyc
# files from a developer's macOS Python sitting in a python:3.12 image. Harmless
# in itself (CPython ignores bytecode tagged for another version, and the stages
# run the filter as a script) but it made the image's contents depend on whatever
# happened to have been run before the build.
#
# Note the `**/`: unlike .gitignore, a bare `__pycache__/` here matches only at
# the context root, not at any depth. That is exactly the mistake this file was
# first written with.
#
# If a future Dockerfile needs another path from the repo, add a `!` line for it.

# 1. nothing by default -- this also keeps .git out of the build context
*

# 2. the only thing the image actually needs
!agentic/

# 3. artefacts that can appear inside agentic/
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp
